---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeRadixText from '@/components/tools/dev/DevEncodeRadixText.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="hex-to-ascii">
  <DevEncodeRadixText client:load radix="hex" direction="decode" asciiFlavour />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Strictly one byte per character</h2>
    <p>
      This hex to ASCII converter uses the classic reading by default: each pair of hex digits is one
      character code from the ASCII table. Textbooks, embedded protocol documentation, CTF puzzles and
      legacy systems use this reading. The hex-to-text page uses UTF-8 by default, where one character
      can span up to four bytes.
    </p>

    <h2>The ASCII ranges worth memorising</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Hex range</th><th>Decimal</th><th>Contents</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>00–1F</td><td>0–31</td><td>Control codes: 09 tab, 0A line feed, 0D carriage return, 1B escape</td></tr>
          <tr><td>20</td><td>32</td><td>Space</td></tr>
          <tr><td>21–2F</td><td>33–47</td><td>Punctuation: <code>!"#$%&amp;'()*+,-./</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>30–39</td><td>48–57</td><td>Digits 0–9 (the digit is the low nibble)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>41–5A</td><td>65–90</td><td>A–Z</td></tr>
          <tr><td>61–7A</td><td>97–122</td><td>a–z, exactly 0x20 above the capitals</td></tr>
          <tr><td>7F</td><td>127</td><td>Delete</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Two patterns come from that layout. The ASCII code of a digit is <code>3</code> followed by the
      digit, so <code>37</code> is <code>7</code>. Case is a single bit: <code>41</code> is
      <code>A</code> and <code>61</code> is <code>a</code>. Add or subtract 0x20 to change it.
    </p>

    <h2>Bytes above 7F</h2>
    <p>
      True ASCII stops at 7F. A higher byte depends on the encoding of the source:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Latin-1 and Windows-1252 use one byte per character: <code>E9</code> is <code>é</code>.</li>
      <li>In UTF-8, the byte is part of a multi-byte sequence: <code>C3 A9</code> is <code>é</code>.</li>
      <li>In another code page, the same byte has a different meaning.</li>
      <li>The data may be binary and not text.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If your decoded output looks like <code>Ã©</code> or <code>â€™</code>, you are reading UTF-8
      bytes one at a time. Switch the character set.
    </p>

    <h2>Working with hex dumps</h2>
    <p>
      A dump from <code>xxd</code>, <code>hexdump -C</code> or Wireshark has three columns: an
      offset, the hex bytes, and an ASCII rendering with dots for unprintable characters. Only the
      middle column is data. If you paste the offsets too, they decode as extra characters, and each
      line of the decoded string starts with wrong characters.
    </p>

    <h2>Control characters in the output</h2>
    <p>
      Bytes below 0x20 have no visible glyph, so a decoded string that has them can look shorter than
      it is or break across lines. Often you decode to find exactly these: a stray <code>0D</code>, an
      embedded <code>1B</code> escape sequence, or a <code>00</code> terminator in what should be plain
      text.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
